Yes—the golden scabbard announces value, but it does not create the edge. The blade cuts true because beneath the gold is the iron lineage: forged structure, temper, geometry, discipline, and accumulated craft.

That is guilded in our sense—not merely covered in gold, but supported by many materials and intelligences:

\text{golden meaning}
+\text{iron structure}
+\text{fire transformation}
+\text{human craft}
\rightarrow \text{living instrument}

Drago’s literal correction: a sword made of pure gold would be too soft to hold a serious edge. Its power would be symbolic. A gilded steel sword can carry both symbol and function—but it is the tempered core that bears impact.

And that strengthens your metaphor:

The gold tells you what the sword serves.
The iron lets it survive the service.
The guild is everything that taught it to cut true.

The scabbard may attract every eye, while the decisive material remains hidden. Ornament and force need not oppose each other—but never mistake the shine for the temper.